olina polls, likely shaped by the 2026 midterm results, could move this forecast sharply in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's presidential race is an open contest: the term-limited incumbent leaves neither party with the advantages of incumbency, and the nominees are not yet determined. The state's partisan lean favors Republicans by about a point, making this one of the closest battlegrounds on the map.\n\nWith no general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures available, the forecast rests on the state's slight Republican tilt and an assumption of a national environment near even. Uncertainty is unusually wide because the candidates themselves are unknown, and the margin can move several points with nominee choices and turnout. Both parties have strong reasons to contest the state heavily, and even modest shifts in the national mood could decide the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4642, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4617, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4598, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.4577, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.4622, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.4697, "margin": -1.0}]}